The '''Third Mahik War''' was fought from 1614 to 1618 between the [[Timara]] chiefdoms of the country south and south-east of [[Hachinawa]] and a combined force of the empire and the [[Sinchew Colony]]. It ended in the defeat of the Timara and the partition of their country between the two victors under the [[Treaty of Tamsui (1618)|Treaty of Tamsui]] of 1618.

The campaign was conducted principally by Hachinawa. The empire provided about 20,000 men and the colony about 1,000, and the colony's share of the conquered territory was half. The war is the third of the campaigns recorded in the colonial period against the peoples of the interior, and it followed the pattern of the [[Second Mahik War]]: the reduction of the country was carried out village by village, and the population was driven out of it rather than incorporated. Losses among the Timara are estimated at about 12,000 dead, enslaved and captured, a figure low in relation to their population because the chiefdoms of the interior withdrew into the forest and were left there rather than pursued.

== Background ==

The area to the south and south-east of Hachinawa was held by Timara chiefdoms rather than by a single polity. Its interior was forest and its margins were the shores of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]], the great northern lake, and the empire had maintained a long frontier with it and tributary relationships across it.

Both the empire and the colony claimed parts of the country. The colony's claim followed from its settlement of the northern coast; the empire's followed from the frontier. The two conferred in 1614 and agreed to divide the territory between them, and the campaign was undertaken to enforce the division against the chiefdoms that held it.

== Forces ==

Hachinawa provided about 20,000 men and lost about 1,300 of them. The colony provided about 1,000, a contingent of the colonial army corps and of the militia raised from the settled districts, and lost about 120. Neither figure is recorded in the period, and both are estimates from the accounts of the two victors.

The Timara were organised in chiefdoms and had no single command. They fought in defence of their own settlements and did not concentrate against the invading force, and the numbers they could raise in any one place were small in relation to the army opposed to them.

== Course of the war ==

The campaign was conducted village by village, and it took four years to complete. The empire's force advanced through the forest and along the lake shore, reducing the settlements in its path and driving the population out of them. The colonial contingent was employed in the south-western part of the country adjoining the colony's own territory, and its operations were on a smaller scale than the empire's.

The Timara did not meet the invading force in the field. They withdrew before it, defended their settlements where the ground permitted, and returned to reoccupy ground that had been cleared once the force had moved on. The war was therefore prolonged by the necessity of clearing the country more than once, and the army was occupied throughout the four years.

The colonial accounts of the campaign are brief and record the succession of settlements reduced rather than the course of operations. The Hachina record is fuller, and it describes a war of movement through forest against an enemy that would not stand.

=== 1614–1616 ===

The campaign opened in 1614 and the empire's force advanced along the lake shore and into the forest to the south of it. By the end of 1616 the greater part of the country had been traversed and its settlements reduced.

=== 1617 ===

The third year was occupied in clearing the country again, the Timara having returned to ground that had been cleared in the first two years. The colonial contingent completed its operations in the south-western districts during this year.

=== 1618 ===

The last of the Timara chiefdoms submitted or was destroyed in 1618, and the war ended. The two victors concluded the [[Treaty of Tamsui (1618)|Treaty of Tamsui]] in the same year, dividing the country between them and fixing the frontier along the line agreed in 1614.

== Aftermath ==

The Timara country was divided between the two victors. Hachinawa took the greater part, which adjoined its own frontier along its whole length, and the colony took the portion adjoining its own territory. Under the boundary of 1618 Hachinawa held about 1,581,000 km² and the colony about 600,000 km², and the empire's share was the largest single extension of its territory.

The Timara population. No provision concerning the Timara was made in the treaty, and none of the chiefdoms was a party to it. The population was driven from the country during the campaign and its position afterwards was determined by the practice of the two victors rather than by the agreement. In the colony the Timara entered the class recorded in the returns as free natives; in the empire they were treated as a subject people of the frontier.

== Casualties ==

The losses of the two victors were about 1,300 men for Hachinawa and about 120 for the colony. The losses of the Timara are estimated at about 12,000 dead, enslaved and captured over the four years of the campaign. No count was made in the period and no record of the dead was kept by either victor, and the figure is an estimate constructed from the colonial and Hachina accounts together.

The figure is low in relation to the population of the country, and the reason is geographical. The Timara population was concentrated along the shores of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]] and in the settled districts, which were the country the campaign traversed. The Timara of the interior withdrew into the forest ahead of the advancing force and were not followed. The army had neither the numbers nor the supply to operate in the deep forest against an enemy that would not stand, and the settlements it could not reach were left alone.

The consequence is that the campaign depopulated the lake country and the accessible margins while leaving the greater part of the Timara population in the interior. The chiefdoms of the forest were neither defeated nor disarmed by the war, and their relations with the two victors continued after it.

== After the war ==

The treaty transferred the country and made no provision for the population. Timara chiefdoms that had withdrawn into the interior descended on the lakeside settlements in the decades that followed, raiding for goods and for stock and attacking the settlers established on the shores. The raids continued through the seventeenth century and were a standing condition of the frontier rather than a response to any particular measure.

The settlement of the lake country was accordingly confined to ground that could be defended, and the interior was left to the chiefdoms that had withdrawn into it. The position of the two victors on the frontier differed: the colony maintained garrisons and a militia in its portion, and the empire held its portion as a subject frontier with the tributary relationships that had preceded the war.

== Historiography ==

The campaign was not described as a war in the colonial records of the period, in which it appears as a series of operations against the peoples of the southern frontier. The Hachina account treats it as a war and is the fuller of the two records. The name by which it is now known places it in sequence with the [[First Mahik War]] of 1538 to 1542 and the [[Second Mahik War]] of 1549 to 1558.

